WEBVTT

00:00:00.708 --> 00:00:07.973
All right. I want to welcome you all to this event's topic, which is Jung's thoughts and ideas about children.

00:00:10.872 --> 00:00:32.900
Something that is not so well known, but he has written about, and there are even specific books I can point towards, which is quite nice to have a topic that, where Jung stays on topic because he really likes to go off on tangents and tries to combine everything all the time. And there's one book from the Collected Works, which is called The Development of Personality.

00:00:33.586 --> 00:00:56.783
And this is on the papers on child psychology, education, and related subjects. So this is a really good introduction if you want to read something on Jung's idea. It's also not so thick, but super interesting that Jung talked and thought about many, many different things. And developmental psychology was also something of that.

00:00:56.831 --> 00:01:17.896
So Jung participated in the beginning of psychology when it was a new science and a really new field, and he dipped his toes in many, many different topics. So I was surprised and at the same time not so surprised that he also wrote extensively about developmental psychology, I would call it today. So how children's psychologies functions and is developing over time.

00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:40.812
And there's another book. Which was published only, I think, 10 years ago or something like this, and it's called Children's Dreams. This one is a little bit bigger, and this deals with a seminar that Jung gave between 1936 and 1940 exclusively on the dreams that children have. And of course, he dabbles there in the psychology of children, and it's super interesting.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:02:00.919
So if you're interested in dream psychology, I can also highly recommend it because it's one of the few books where he actually explains how he analyzes dreams, which he does not do in the Collected Works. We only found out about this much later. He talks about it mainly in the seminars. Yeah.

00:02:02.018 --> 00:02:18.743
So what does Jung talk about when he talks about children and their psychology? We have to call into our memory how Jung sees the psyche. And this, in the psyche, Jung sees two very big areas.

00:02:19.096 --> 00:02:39.541
And one is consciousness, and one is the unconscious, sometimes labeled the subconscious, not by Jung, but by others. He calls it always very clearly the unconscious. And this is because there's a relationship between consciousness and the unconscious, because everything that is not in consciousness is the unconscious, which is a lot of stuff.

00:02:39.863 --> 00:03:04.108
Because consciousness is basically everything that you know, that you can do, that you can perceive willfully and attentively. So things that you notice and that spring into your eye, and also that you can do in a controlled manner. You can drive a car in a controlled or an uncontrolled way, and only one of those ways is conscious.

00:03:05.954 --> 00:03:29.895
And also healthy. And this unconscious is very big in general, and consciousness is what is unique quirk to human beings, that we have something as consciousness, that it is like a net that is perceiving but also remembering stimuli that are coming from outside or inside.

00:03:30.441 --> 00:03:55.242
So for Jung, memory is an important part of consciousness, the same as will, to willfully do things or not do them, which is a form of control, planned acting, and to have a kind of coherence between one's action that you create a sense of self and of a coherent whole that is related everything to each other.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:04:10.384
When you have consciousness, everything is related to you, the ego as an I. And when you, for example, have a job interview, these are the things that you then talk about. Or when you meet a new person, they say, oh, I, I like this, I'm working as this, I have done this, and so on. It's always this I and the connection to it.

00:04:11.074 --> 00:04:34.754
But of course, everything in the unconscious is not connected to the I, the ego, and also not connected in conscious in general, which is a lot. This is what Jung says. We start out as human beings when we are born as children. There is no consciousness yet. There is no ego. There is no will. Small children do not act willfully. They don't have a plan.

00:04:34.818 --> 00:04:57.259
They can't control themselves. They're pure emotions, impulses, and they just act. And you can see this for the first couple of years that there's also no continuity of any kind. There's just acting and it can change from second to second. Extreme emotions, extreme excitement, extreme sadness, extreme hunger, extreme tiredness.

00:04:58.223 --> 00:05:20.904
And it's only with time that something pops up. And this is when the child sees itself as separated from everything else. And this is where the I developed, the ego, where it's— Jung basically discovered this is something that just pops up into existence. And consciousness starts to form around it.

00:05:21.781 --> 00:05:46.753
This is around the time when children discover the verb no and can exert their will and can see suddenly there's a separation between them and the things that are happening around them. And it is a slow, gradual process that you have the child developing will and developing consciousness, but it takes many, many years.

00:05:46.884 --> 00:06:02.505
This is why children are a long time in education. And it takes a long time for people to then actually participate in society and have their own life and also have their own personality because in the beginning everything is unconscious.

00:06:03.308 --> 00:06:23.482
Carl Gustav Jung talks about this a lot in the sense of self, as he says, the self as a concept is everything that you could be, but this is the future. This is a potential. The groundwork for the future is already in the unconscious, and one pulls it out and connects it to one's consciousness and one's ego.

00:06:24.364 --> 00:06:46.048
And this is what you can see when people evolve and develop, that they get a personality. There's a certain way how they see the world, how they want to act in the world, what they like, what they don't like, what preferences they have. If they are extroverted, if they're introverted, if they're most thinking or more feeling. Or if they're very creative or very diligent.

00:06:46.674 --> 00:07:06.614
And this gets developed and clears out through time. This is what's pulled out of the unconscious and gets connected. It's like a big system where different parts get turned on over time, allowing for more complex behavior and a more complex personality. So the personality of the child is already there, but it is unconscious. This is dormant.

00:07:07.385 --> 00:07:31.968
It needs time to unfold like a flower. And this process for Jung does not necessarily stop with the age of 16 or 18. For him, it is a development that happens the whole life of a person, which he calls individuation. And the goal is the Self. This is the everything that you could be. Your psyche is trying to push you and strives toward that.

00:07:32.273 --> 00:07:48.781
But in the beginning, we're staying with children, they're not all of that. They're just emotions, they're impulses. They are highly susceptible to things happening outside of them, and they're uncontrolled. And Jung says that's the case for all human beings.

00:07:49.038 --> 00:08:13.881
And he says also for human societies, humans as a species, that we have a consciously evolved and formed consciousness. It's something very new. For him, consciousness in the human history sense started with writing when people really externalized things and they have had to write something down, which is then again in a coherent manner, right?

00:08:13.962 --> 00:08:35.967
In a willful and planned manner. People don't write by accident. They don't learn reading and writing by accident. And he said, this is really when human consciousness started in the cultural sense. And from then on, A lot of the human endeavor was to really grow the consciousness of people. And this is education. This is being in schools.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:51.822
That is learning rules, following laws, acting in more complex societies. And this is fairly new. Human beings have not done this for so long. We can count it in thousands of years, but human beings existed for millions of years.

00:08:52.626 --> 00:09:11.599
And he was really interested in this state, this early state that he called primitive and primitive psychology without that being a value judgment, but rather describing a state which is just early. We could call it maybe superstitious or pre-scientific in a modern context.

00:09:12.256 --> 00:09:38.820
And what Carl Gustav Jung was interested in then in children, of course, to see this seeds, this base operating system of human beings that is running in the background, that everything is built on top, not only in children, but also he visited a lot of native people that are of course removed from this cultural process of these complex societies and institutions and so on.

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:45.929
So he traveled a lot to South Africa, so to Africa, to South America, to India, and so on.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:10:11.617
Because he said this is not unusual, like the, this superstitious way of, of living and perceiving the world to say everything is animated and there's certain ways you should behave and that dreams are real and all these things that this is still how we work deep inside. He said our conscious level, our cultural level is like a very thin crust.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:31.559
That can easily be broken. For example, every time we are tired, we are angry, we are under extreme circumstances. And he saw this really breaking in his lifetime multiple times because he lived through the First World War and the Second World War and said, yeah, the humanity that we have, this cultivated conscious layer, it is thin because it is very new.

00:10:32.377 --> 00:10:57.205
And he was interested in children because It was a gateway to really the deeper parts and deeper layers of the psyche because he saw when you go deep enough in the psyche that you hit a layer called the collective unconscious, which is, there is a unconscious, which is in every human being and is connecting everybody.

00:10:58.127 --> 00:11:17.285
We would call it human nature or predispositions where he would say, okay, there's things that just pop up all by themselves all the time. You have in all cultures, in all societies, you have religion, you have stories, and they have very often certain forms and structures and motives that just pop up.

00:11:17.445 --> 00:11:36.982
Like people like to tell stories and it just happens to be always the same stories. They look different, but in their core and their structure, that they're very, very identical. And this collective part is very foreign and hard to understand because it is very, very deep below.

00:11:37.545 --> 00:11:59.467
So he looked at children and especially children's dreams to understand what is happening there in collective unconscious, because same as this primitive psychology, as he called it, the collective unconscious is still there and is acting on us and is pushing things up. He says, this is where cultures come from and creative works of art, and all these things.

00:11:59.515 --> 00:12:20.249
So what we like as humans, it is defined by the collective unconscious, by our human nature. And I have a quote for that where he describes this: Young children have a consciousness which is remarkable. I find the psychology of little children exceedingly difficult. Their dreams, for instance, are amazingly difficult.

00:12:21.244 --> 00:12:41.777
One would assume that they would be quite simple, but but they are far from that. Of course, some are obvious, but they have an unusual, a number of great dreams and great visions too. And to deal with them requires a hypothesis, which makes me quite dizzy. One has to assume that they have a consciousness of the collective unconscious, an amazing thing.

00:12:42.436 --> 00:13:04.424
So he says, children are mainly unconscious, and this means they're very closer to the collective unconscious. And you see it. In their dreams, in the way how they behave, and that this is how every human being inside also behaves, even though that they might not know it or not be aware of it or forgot it or deny it. There's still this old psychology in there.

00:13:05.386 --> 00:13:26.056
And he called children really not blank slates, which is also a stream of, let's say, philosophy, that human beings come into the world as blank slates and you can teach them them and bring them in any form you want through education. Because it might look like this when you have small children, you can tell them everything, you can make them do things.

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:48.964
But as I said, for Jung, there's a personality already there, and this personality wants to express. Same as you have handedness. It's very hard to change the handedness of people, and people tried. I'm left-handed, for example. I could have not gotten into school 50 years ago in Germany and older because they would have tried to make me write with my right hand.

00:13:50.256 --> 00:14:10.958
Of course, we stopped that, but it just goes that human beings, they have a preference that's already there. And they are mainly unconscious about themselves and need to explore themselves. And this is what life journey is about. When children are born and from a mother and father and come into a family.

00:14:12.434 --> 00:14:31.892
Jung looked very closely at these dynamics because for Jung, he worked as a therapist, as a, a psychologist. When he had a patient, he would not only look at the patient themselves, but rather he said, okay, they're ingrained in a kind of network of other people. How are the people around them? How do they react to that person, to his patient?

00:14:33.352 --> 00:14:48.769
And of course he then saw a lot of family relationships, either with the parents of the patient or with the partner and the child of the patient. And this is where he noticed a lot of stuff.

00:14:49.655 --> 00:15:00.553
And one thing that a lot of people are aware of is how people that are related to each other, that they look very similar, that there's certain features that pop up.

00:15:00.902 --> 00:15:28.037
From time to time in, in a family tree, when you look in a family tree and you know a lot of your relatives, like sisters, brothers, nephews, nieces, aunts, and uncles, and there's certain just physical traits that come up, like, oh, there's a certain height, there's certain ear, there's certain eyes or nose, and there's some traits that seem to skip certain generations that suddenly, oh, he looks like his grandfather or great-grandfather.

00:15:29.183 --> 00:15:45.990
Especially now that we have a lot of pictures of people, especially when they were younger, it's quite eerie. Like, I also have people in my family that look identical to people a couple of generations back. And Jung said, this is well known and everybody understands it, but he says the psychology is the same.

00:15:46.620 --> 00:16:06.832
There's a composite happening, not only physiologically, but also psychologically, that there are some psychological traits within a family, that there is a family psychology. And it is this building blocks that get reassembled basically every time when a child is born in a family. And this is the point he makes very clear.

00:16:06.881 --> 00:16:29.269
He says the child is never really the child of the mother and father, but it's the child of the families of the mother and the father. And this is pretty clear, and I think everybody knows it, that children are very different from their parents. But if you look at the family tree, you can say, oh yeah, they, it seems to be belonging together somehow.

00:16:31.586 --> 00:16:45.459
That the child is combining in them the psychology of both families. And Jung even makes a statement that if those psychologies are very different, the psychology of the child can be troubled by that.

00:16:45.893 --> 00:17:04.944
He does not really specify what that means, but he says that a child can basically suffer for being the child of two families. As I said, there's a kind of family psychology, a family spirit that gets incarnated in form of the child.

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:30.869
And he talks about when you have specific neurotic family members, like black sheep, he says, oh, those are the mostly the closest to the family spirit because they are possessed by the family psychology and have very little of their own individuality and says people suffer from that when they can't really live their own life, but are burdened by external forces.

00:17:31.366 --> 00:17:44.302
For example, archetypes, right? Or emotions. When this unfolding of the personality through individuation that's supposed to take place cannot really take place because they're overwhelmed in their consciousness, it cannot develop. As it should be.

00:17:44.933 --> 00:18:08.464
And while people very often look to outside forces to say like, oh, I can't develop as I want to because of my family or society or job or whatever, or the law. Jung says the same is also true psychologically, like that people caught by emotion and neurosis and archetypes that drive their lives.

00:18:08.926 --> 00:18:23.482
And prevent them from developing as they should be and become an individuation, to become a coherent whole. And neurosis is a very good point because it says families not only have a psychology, but also they have neurosis.

00:18:23.948 --> 00:18:46.194
There is generational trauma that can be propagated through different generations that are given through the parents to the child and This you can also see in some, some families that there's a struggling happening that just propagates through the generations until somebody says, okay, I want to break out of it.

00:18:46.485 --> 00:19:07.329
It has to be a conscious, willful choice and also a lot of effort to move beyond that. But this is what it takes to become an individual and become oneself. So. This already goes pretty far. I, I wanted to start with the, with the start of life.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:29.712
So when the child is born with its already in place psychology, in a sense that will unfold over time, at first will be not itself, but rather an extension of the psychology of the mother. This is what Jung noticed a lot. The same as a child is an extension of the body. Of the mother, right? It's growing out of the body of the mother.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:51.950
The psychology is also first completely entrenched and connected to the psychology of the mother. And this is of course a great task of children when they grew up, then later in life to get out of this family psychological atmosphere and to become their own persons. This is what Freud dealt with.

00:19:52.576 --> 00:20:10.688
Freud dealt with the people in the beginning of their life that should go out of the basement of their parents, but don't want to. And they suffer for it because in the background, there's still the personality, there's still the life, there's a Self, and it's pushing the person out. And if it does not want to do it, it suffers and it gets neurosis.

00:20:10.897 --> 00:20:28.313
It becomes, it clings to this infantile state and does not want to do anything. Self-sufficient in the world, and the person becomes neurotic. And of course, in the beginning, this is just how it is.

00:20:28.668 --> 00:20:43.225
The child is still, uh, freshly born and freshly new, and it is an extension of the psychology of the mother and to an extent also of the psychology of the father. This is what he talks about really a lot.

00:20:44.116 --> 00:21:03.747
That the psychology of the parents have a huge influence on the child, and it is way more influential as than what the parents are saying. He says what parents do or any educator does is way more important to the child than what they say.

00:21:04.164 --> 00:21:18.427
Saying and speaking is very often a conscious thing, but what we do and what we act is also very unconscious. So the things we do not want to have slip out, very often they show themselves in our actions. This is why the saying goes, watch the hands, not the mouth.

00:21:19.572 --> 00:21:37.708
And he says, and this is already pretty smart parental advice, the child will follow what it sees and perceives there. And he says children are very, very perceptive. And this is because the unconscious ever thinks is also very perceptive.

00:21:40.090 --> 00:22:04.815
The same as consciousness can see things and react to those things, he says the unconscious also sees things and reacts to those things. Something that Freud, for example, said: when we dream of something, the biggest influence what we see in a dream is what we've perceived the day before we slept. But it's those things that we maybe shortly perceived and immediately forget.

00:22:06.112 --> 00:22:20.181
And they sneak through the back door into our psyche and, and our unconscious and stay there. There's the things that we immediately forget that we have thousands of millions of impressions every second, every minute, all day.

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:41.469
There's always stuff in the background that's only there for a very short moment and it's all gets collected in the unconscious and then comes out during dreaming. And the same way children react to these things that they perceive, and they cannot really control them. That makes them very honest. And they're perceiving in a big part to the psychology of the parents.

00:22:41.983 --> 00:23:02.384
So everything Jung says that the parents in themselves have not taken care of their shadow, the part where they do not want to go, and they have not dealt with their neurosis, their, their trauma, their unsolved things. It all will play out in the child first.

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:28.713
He says this multiple times, that children are living out the unlived life of the parents. So everything that parents have not taken care of in themselves, in their life, in their psychology, in their development, it will catch the child immediately, and it will be a kind of possession. This will be then the same as an archetype or an emotion gripping the child.

00:23:28.777 --> 00:23:50.945
It will define the life of the child and of course make it neurotic, because again, it's not then the child's life, but rather the unlived life of the parents that is trying to express itself. And this is why he makes a statement that he says children can never be neurotic. Because they don't have a reason for a neurosis.

00:23:51.201 --> 00:24:11.686
Jung saw neurosis really as a kind of healing mechanism. The same way when you have a virus infection that your body temperature rises and you get a headache and all those things. The temperature rising and the headache and the stuffy nose and the coughing, that's not the disease. The disease is the virus and all those symptoms are there to kill the virus.

00:24:12.375 --> 00:24:35.023
And Jung sees the psyche in the same way. A neurosis. Is a healing attempt. It is there to make the person aware of something that they do not want to be aware of, or they are not aware of, but should be. So it is like heat coming up and you have to ask yourself, okay, why I'm so hot? Oh, maybe I'm ill. But psychologically is, oh, why am I without any energy?

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:50.480
And why am I unfocused? And why can't I live my life normally? Oh, there's maybe something I have not done that I should have done. This is Jung's idea about how the psyche works and how the the unconscious pushes things to us, how the Self, our future us, is pushing things to us.

00:24:51.795 --> 00:25:10.949
This is why I said when children act neurotic, this is not the neurosis of the child because the child has no disconnect yet. The disconnect is very often associated with a consciousness which is adapted and evolved in the wrong or incomplete direction. And children do not have yet this kind of developed consciousness.

00:25:11.707 --> 00:25:34.247
And he would say, okay, the first thing he would look at when he would see a neurotic child would be the mother, as the child is an extension of the psychology of the mother. And he then would very closely look into the life of the mother and the parents and how they are and whether they have a problem.

00:25:34.651 --> 00:25:51.032
And he would notice when he would solve the problem of the parents, the symptoms of the child will disappear. Because again, the child is not willfully doing anything. It is close to the unconscious and the unconscious is reacting to what it is perceiving. And the perceiving is these unresolved problems and this unresolved trauma.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:26:15.559
He very often would also use this as a diagnostic tool. When you, for example, would have a father as a patient, he would talk to the child and ask child, what they're dreaming. The ideas of Jung regarding dreams are very complex and pretty far, so I can highly recommend the episode what I, that I did on there.

00:26:15.721 --> 00:26:37.054
But the basic idea is really that the unconscious is reacting to consciousness and is trying to make consciousness aware of the thing that is not aware of. And The child then perceives those things and the same things constellate in their unconscious and express themselves then also in their dreams.

00:26:37.953 --> 00:26:56.639
And Jung would, would do that pretty often to look in the vicinity of people with psychological problems, what kinds of dreams they would have. And he, this seems to be that it was not unusual because he had one colleague who really would do that to bring patients to their villa and they were living remotely.

00:26:57.153 --> 00:27:15.841
And let them stay there overnight and then talk to their own family that was living there and also the, the housekeepers about their dreams that they have in the night, because your unconscious is incredibly perceptive. It's like the collective wisdom of human beings in the background, this collective part that has seen everything.

00:27:17.239 --> 00:27:34.400
When something is in the collective unconscious, it means it happens so often as a pattern, as a situation. That it became ingrained genetically in human nature to watch out for that and perceive that. This is why you can react in very dangerous situations so quickly.

00:27:34.496 --> 00:27:54.034
It's not you willfully saying, oh, I will now move to the side, but rather your body, your psyche is already acting before you notice that. And you have this really in extreme situations. So similar, the unconscious is very perceptive and would push them in dreams and other people what they, other people in their vicinity should dream.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:28:18.544
Because he would do that mainly when people would have not dreams on their own, even though they should have. And then he would poke around and find it in the children that of course are living out the unlived life of the parents. And there I have a very nice quote. Man usually treats the children as he treats the woman and as he treats his own feelings.

00:28:20.294 --> 00:28:43.208
This goes back to the idea, okay, how complete are you as a human being and how are you taking care of the things that are already inside of you? And Jung has this idea about projecting, which means everything you do not take care of in yourself and you don't want to see, see, and you don't want to, to engage with, that you see it projected in the outside world.

00:28:44.777 --> 00:28:59.946
He said in primitive psychology, this was just how people were because they were very unconscious. So they saw their old psychology in the outside world. They see gods in, and just animated things in rocks and rivers in the sky.

00:29:00.629 --> 00:29:24.378
And this still happens to us that we, when we don't want to see or associate ourselves with certain things. That we perceive them outside all the more because our unconscious again tries to steer us in that direction and say like, hey, pay attention to that, do something with that. But of course the reaction is then of course negative, like, oh, I hate that person or this thing.

00:29:25.357 --> 00:29:48.597
And people don't want to engage with that. And this is how they keep their malformed, maladapted consciousness. So for Jung, just mother and father themselves, and this is a big topic, and there was a huge disagreement between him and Freud.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:30:06.505
For Freud, the mother and father, the mother and father complex, so the, the baggage basically people get from their parents consciously or unconsciously, is defined by the actual parents, the actual mother and the actual father.

00:30:07.781 --> 00:30:30.936
But again, we are talking about Jung and he says certain situations and experiences, when they happen often enough, they are in the collective unconscious. And of course, having a mother and father is something archetypal. This is the truth for all human beings that ever existed, which means the idea of a mother and father is already there. There's a kind of reference point.

00:30:31.001 --> 00:30:46.580
There's already an idea in the child how mother and father should be. And of course, of course, a deviation from that ideal causes then also potential stress to the, to the child and can create problems there.

00:30:47.857 --> 00:31:05.148
Because it's not like when a child only knows a mother that it does not have an idea what a father is, but rather it has a very clear idea what a father is. But only in this archetypal, abstract, eternal way, but not in this concretized, as a real person father way.

00:31:05.229 --> 00:31:21.395
And you can see that in people, for example, when they grow up without a father, that they try to find the father figure somewhere else. I have friends like this that you, they're suddenly interested in certain personalities very often in public life in a, let's say, very extreme way.

00:31:21.962 --> 00:31:43.567
Because there is this need and to express this father archetype somewhere, to find it somewhere. And of course, this can have this whole lot of other problems that can be created by that. Yeah. One second.

00:31:45.793 --> 00:32:02.977
So when we look into children and we look into education, and I talked about it in the sense of, oh, an educator or parent should be aware of the things they have unsolved in their own psyche because it's not gone. It's not gone from their own life, but it's also then deeply affecting their children.

00:32:03.315 --> 00:32:18.877
This is the number one advice he gives to educators, like, okay, deal with your unresolved things. How to basically help and educate children and He talks a lot about fairy tales, of course, because it's Jung.

00:32:19.166 --> 00:32:43.048
He likes stories, mythological, religious, spiritual, occult, and also fairy tales, because for him, these are codifications of this collective unconscious. This means these experiences that are eternal and are coming up all by themselves have to manifest themselves somehow. Because it's strange. It's like a completely strange situation.

00:32:44.450 --> 00:33:13.755
It's, let's say, in the outside world, because it's always easier to understand in an outside world, an archetypal situation is always like, okay, you want to find a partner and you're on your first date, for example, or you become a parent yourself, or you come into a new group, or you join Yeah, a company, which would be a modern thing, but goes back to this idea of being in a group or being in, in danger and all these things.

00:33:14.062 --> 00:33:34.665
This is archetypal outside, but also inside things can come up, which are archetypal, like this idea of the shadow or the anima or the Self, or just certain patterns that repeat themselves that put people on rails and to express and to digest these things.

00:33:35.163 --> 00:33:58.028
He says fairy tales show the relationship between certain concepts. So for us, it's pretty clear that the Greek gods were expressing human emotions and how Greek gods were related to each other showed how certain emotions are relating to each other and how they interact with each other. So it's already an exploration of psychology for Jung. These stories.

00:33:59.344 --> 00:34:17.215
Mythology, religion, spiritual, and so on, are exploration of the psyche to say, okay, how does the psyche process things? How does the psyche evolve? What is happening there in the background? And he said, this is why it's so important to give children fairy tales. And we have fairy tales that are hundreds of thousands of years old.

00:34:17.568 --> 00:34:27.110
And so again, always the same stories and people like them because they're expressing something and they're activating something in the psyche, which is dormant or was dormant before.

00:34:29.459 --> 00:34:51.104
And the same way as children have fairy tales, he says, like, adults need a religious structure, a spiritual structure, something that, that helps them order their life and order their emotions and the tasks that they need to do to make sense of it, to really channel it. He sees it as when things come up, it's like a raw material. It needs to be transformed.

00:34:51.722 --> 00:35:05.764
In something useful and good. Same as anger itself. It's an emotion. It's archetypal. Like everybody knows it. It comes all by itself. You can see it in children. Children are most of the time very angry. They even measured that.

00:35:06.069 --> 00:35:25.919
I saw a study somewhere that, for example, when the child wants its mother, which is perfectly normal for a very small child, and the father comes in, that the child starts to cry. Not because it's sad, because it's angry. And this anger, of course, stays with people their whole life.

00:35:26.128 --> 00:35:43.735
And what Carl Gustav Jung was looking towards is, okay, this primal emotion that is always there, what are human beings doing with it? And you can learn how to control your anger, not to suppress it, but rather to channel it into certain things. For example, be energetic or be competitive, for example, in sports.

00:35:44.228 --> 00:36:03.527
It's a very good thing if it can facilitate your anger in a productive, healthy manner that does not harm you and others in the long term. But being in a game, for example, and being in a competitive setting, like anger is something very useful as long as someone is not losing their head. Same as you want to find a partner.

00:36:03.848 --> 00:36:17.693
There are, let's say, quick and illegal ways how to get sex with someone, and we throw people in prison for that, and that for very good reason. But there's also courtship. There's a degree of sophistication that people are learning and that children are learning.

00:36:17.741 --> 00:36:32.035
And he says to make sense and structure this, this is what stories are for, not to indoctrinate children and saying, oh, this is how the world is and how you should perceive the world, but rather to structure and order the things that are already inside of them and that will come up.

00:36:33.223 --> 00:36:54.928
And he says it's perfectly human for human beings to grow up and learn a lot of things and they cannot connect them yet, but with time they will understand. I had this really strongly in, in school, the German classes. We read a lot of German classics and they do not make sense to children.

00:36:55.121 --> 00:37:12.805
There are a lot of books where I have to say, okay, it doesn't make sense that a child of 14 or 12 should read this book. Not because it's a bad book, not because it's bad topics, but rather The life experience for understanding it is missing. So there needs to be kind of foundation again, this thing of connection of consciousness.

00:37:12.997 --> 00:37:31.158
I need to have experienced certain things that make me wonder and I say, okay, I, I don't know how to fit this puzzle pieces together that then a story can come in and like an explanation and say, and order it and make it suddenly make sense. Like a stroke of insight, like in a murder mystery when the twist comes and it's revealed who the real murderer is.

00:37:31.254 --> 00:37:46.312
You have all these Strange disconnected things in the beginning, but then comes this stroke of insight and then everything falls into place. And this is how consciousness basically evolves by accumulating things, but then we structure them constantly.

00:37:47.451 --> 00:38:07.712
And this is why children have to learn a lot of things that they can't make sense of and being told, especially by their parents, a lot of things that they just follow as rules in the beginning. And it's important that they learn rules first to really foster consciousness. So Jung saw human beings as rule-generating beings.

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:24.755
That's just what people like archetypally to do, but he saw they're also the foundation of culture because rules always, always means you need to consciously do things or not do things that you have to keep them in mind. So having a taboo, for example, to not do things that things are sacred and so on.

00:38:25.706 --> 00:38:48.584
He saw that really as a stepping stone to develop consciousness because you have to pay attention and you have to watch your actions and you have to have this continuity. This is why Jung is so interesting because he's connecting really everything. We are talking about the beginning of human civilization and human beings, children, teenagers, they accumulate all these things.

00:38:48.632 --> 00:39:08.917
They can't make sense. And he says, this is what's happened in the then in the second part of life, when people go past the age of 38 or 40, that they discover their inner world, that they discover culture, and they discover the meaning of things. Before it was field research, he calls that, and then there's really life beginning.

00:39:11.026 --> 00:39:34.518
And yeah, this is of course only after the children actually leave the children's psychology. But it would not be Jung if it would not be a little bit more complicated, because first you are a child, you try not to be a child anymore, you try to be an adult. But he says at a certain point you have to become a child again, and I have a quote for that.

00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:54.862
For instance, you remember in Symbols of Transformation, I say the Christian teaching is that you must sacrifice your own childishness, for how can you become like unto a child when you are still a child. First you must overcome your childishness, and then after you have been an adult, you can become a child again. This is very clearly the teaching of Christ.

00:39:57.240 --> 00:40:21.907
For Jung, life and living experience is always this oscillating between poles. So you should always go there where you are not currently. So what is your strong suit? You should go to the weak suit. It's always oscillating between things, between opposites, between poles to then find a middle path and become complete.

00:40:23.876 --> 00:40:34.193
And this is where I would end my presentation with these words. And I want to thank you for your attention, and I hope you learned something. And yeah.

